&lt;h2 id="garden-description"&gt;Garden Description&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A broken tombstone from Myra &lt;!--[(Petersen-Luschan, Reisen II 36,56)](https://inscriptions.packhum.org/text/283424?hs=146-150%2C178-183)--&gt;records part of a testator’s instructions to certain persons (gardeners?) charged with “not living elsewhere but staying in the heroon and themselves taking care of it completely” (&lt;em&gt;ἀλλαχῆ οἰκῆσαι, μένειν δὲ ἐν τῶ ἡρώω καὶ αὐτοὺς ποιεῖσθαι τὴν ἐπιμέλειαν αὐτοῦ πᾶσαν&lt;/em&gt;) in return for which “they are to have use of the walled garden and the buildings in it, two underground rooms, and the two corresponding rooms on the ground floor” (&lt;em&gt;οἳ καὶ ἐχέτωσαν τὴν χρῆσιν τοῦ περιτετειχισμένου κηπίου καὶ τῶν ἐν αὐτῷ οἰκημάτων, οἴκων καταγείων δύο· καὶ τῶν κατ’ αὐτῶν ἀναγείων δύο&lt;/em&gt;). The two “houses” (&lt;em&gt;oikoi&lt;/em&gt;) were probably humble structures, combining storage rooms for garden equipment and residential space for the gardeners.&lt;/p&gt;
